Z||000029||TI_3VOM||10-04-2001||03:46 PM||||hhhmmm...e-mail Bob Lutz....bring back Fiero e-mail campaign....sounds familiar. (O/T section)<P>Seriously, though, if any of you are serious about this, I'd add some of these points;<BR>a) How can Pontiac be GM's performance division without a performance car? (Firebird goes bye-bye in '02)<BR>b) If there is no room at GM for a niche vehicle, then explain the Corvette.<BR>c) When the Fiero first came out, it was also a jump off point for the (then) new style that Pontiac was to bring to the rest of the line. Given that overly "Gotham" stylings of late, wouldn't this also be an opportune time to point Pontiacs' style in a new direction? Could the new Fiero serve this purpose?<BR>d) Just what do we have to compete with the Miatas and MR-2s out there?<BR>...and finally, <BR>e) Do not bother to call it a Fiero if it is not a two seat, mid-engined, space-frame built sports car.<P>...just my two-cents worth.<P>P.S. Love the idea of inviting him to Fiero gatherings. Excellent, really. Since he loves to fly as well (owns his own L-39 trainer jet), how about one one the same weekend as an airshow? Wings & wheels!! TIA ||||reg||4
Z||000030||BN Boomer||10-05-2001||04:47 AM||||I think you can forget about the Fiero coming back any time soon. Altough undeserved, the Fiero simply has too bad a reputation to overcome and it's target audience is just too small. A company can only afford to keep so many niche vehicles in production and I don't see the Corvette being dropped any time soon.  <BR>I wonder how many of those who think GM should squander it's resources on low profit, high risk vehicles actually own any GM stock or if any of those folks have other investments and expect the highest returns possible. That is who GM is trying to satisfy.<P>As for the return of the Impala, I just bought one and beg to differ with those who ignorantly claim that it is the opposite of what it "originally stood for". What kind of crap is that? How many of you ever even owned one of the older Impalas? The new Impala offers exactly what the original one did back in the 50s; a family car loaded with amenities at an attractive, affordable price.   ||||reg||1
Z||000031||jeffreyeffrey||10-05-2001||12:12 PM||||Let's face it, these days people don't buy Corvettes, Camaro's, and Mustangs to keep up with the Jones's, they go and buy $40,000 Pickups and Suv's. For some reason a slow, large, gas guzzleing tank is more appealing to Joe T. Consumer right now than a quick high performance Sports car. So I'm pretty sure that if the Fiero does come back it will not be a contender to the Corvette. However let's remember what triggerd the development of the first Fiero. Remember?.......... Fuel conservation. Now if Gasoline prices keep as high as they have in the last year and a half, fuel efficiency is going to be as big of topic in the auto industry as it was in the late 70s early 80s. So perhaps Fiero will Resurrect as what it was origianlly released as, a fun commuter car. Pontiac would also have to make damn sure it's done right this time, because critics will no doubt be watching like hawks waiting to see Fiero's reputation damaged more than it already is. In my opinion if Pontiac would dust off the 88GT molds and just come out with the same car, Its modern enough looking yet that I bet they'd sell a million.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Fformula88||10-05-2001||12:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jeffreyeffrey:<BR><B>Let's face it, these days people don't buy Corvettes, Camaro's, and Mustangs to keep up with the Jones's, they go and buy $40,000 Pickups and Suv's. For some reason a slow, large, gas guzzleing tank is more appealing to Joe T. Consumer right now than a quick high performance Sports car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Got that right!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  Its a little messed up, isn't it?<P>Ok, I can understand the practical side to a point, but whats so practical about a leather lined, 10 MPG luxury-barge SUV that you would be scared to carry a peice of plywood in for fear of tearing the nice cowhides!  Give me a sports car and a cheap "practical" vehicle (if needed) anyday!||||reg||1
Z||000033||GTFiero1||10-05-2001||04:22 PM||||As for the return of the Impala, I just bought one and beg to differ with those who ignorantly claim that it is the opposite of what it "originally stood for". What kind of crap is that? How many of you ever even owned one of the older Impalas? The new Impala offers exactly what the original one did back in the 50s; a family car loaded with amenities at an attractive, affordable price. <P>Nah my dad had a 1994 Impala SS (only 6,303 made)everything thats on the caprice cop cars roaming around your town this car has. Plus edelbrock headers, intake, cat back exhaust, cold air intake, mass air-flow sensor and some more. This was a cool car! then one day the car got totaled. its gone now and we have an 87 cadillac Coupe Deville we got from my grandparents in florida. it is in absolute mint condition but does not compare to the impala. when the 94 SS was in the shop he rented a 200 Impala LS and he hateded it. it was at least a half-foot narrower, front drive with only 200hp and pretty damn small for a full size car. hopefully they bring back the rear-wheel drive for 2005 as planned. for any of you wanting a high performance rear drive full size car check out this site. <A HREF="http://www.impalaclub.com" TARGET=_blank>www.impalaclub.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000034||jeffreyeffrey||10-05-2001||04:54 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by GTFiero1:<BR>[B]As for the return of the Impala, I just bought one and beg to differ with those who ignorantly claim that it is the opposite of what it "originally stood for". What kind of crap is that? How many of you ever even owned one of the older Impalas? The new Impala offers exactly what the original one did back in the 50s; a family car loaded with amenities at an attractive, affordable price. <P>There is no question that the new impala is a lovely car, however it is not exactly a one to one comparison with the origianal. Now people complain when a former performance name is resurrected as a front drive, But bread and butter sedans seem to get away with it. Take models like bonneville, park avenue, lesabre. These names have never been discontinued, they've changed with the times and people don't seem to yell about the front wheel drive v-6 aspect of these cars. The problem with Impala is its former SS distinction. People assosiate that name with performance. When I look at the new Impala I can't see burning up broadway in a 4 door front drive v6. I think that's what people mean by not living up to it's name.  <P>Pontiac seems to have enough respect for themselves to not bring back discontinued names on dissapointing models. (with exception to their worst imbarassment, last le mans)They realize folks develop kinships with their automobiles of old, and won't settle for anything but the original platforms. Cheverolet however has been throwing around their classic names so much I'm loosing respect for the company, Malibu isn't a malibu its a corsica, Monte carlo isn't a Monte it's a Lumina, and Impala although nice is no Impala in my book. Imagine if Pontiac would stamp the letters GTO on the front grill of the current Grand Prix! or Fiero came back as a front engine front drive! Hold on to your hats! People would throw a fit from here to high heaven, myself included.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000035||BN Boomer||10-05-2001||05:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTFiero1:<BR>Nah my dad had a 1994 Impala SS (only 6,303 made)everything thats on the caprice cop cars roaming around your town this car has. Plus edelbrock headers, intake, cat back exhaust, cold air intake, mass air-flow sensor and some more. This was a cool car! then one day the car got totaled. its gone now and we have an 87 cadillac Coupe Deville we got from my grandparents in florida. it is in absolute mint condition but does not compare to the impala. when the 94 SS was in the shop he rented a 200 Impala LS and he hateded it. it was at least a half-foot narrower, front drive with only 200hp and pretty damn small for a full size car. hopefully they bring back the rear-wheel drive for 2005 as planned. for any of you wanting a high performance rear drive full size car check out this site. <A HREF="http://www.impalaclub.com" TARGET=_blank>www.impalaclub.com</A> <BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hate to be the one to break it you, but the 94-96 Impala SS was NOT typical of the traditional Impala series. It was, in fact, an anomaly. I grew up with a couple of Impalas, a 63 and a 74 to be exact, and you are sadly mistaken if you think they were performance cars, especially by the 70s. <P>Ya just gotta love how everyone says GM finally got the Impala right with the 94-96 SS yet the funny thing is, not many people ran out and bought them. Go figure. Even your own dad apparently didn't think it was a good enough car to replace with an identical one.<P>Granted the new Impala might not be as large as the older ones, but it's still a heck of a lot roomier than most other new cars, especially anything anywhere near it's price range. With the front bucket seats, I actually love how much legroom and headroom it has. I wouldn't count on seeing a RWD Impala any time soon, either. Maybe a SC3800 or small V-8, but I think the FWD is here to stay. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||Rick 88||10-05-2001||05:45 PM||||If Toyota is selling new MR2's, Honda is selling S-2000's, and Mazda is still selling the Miata, why can't Pontiac produce a new Fiero. The Vibe is not going to make it as an image car. Not everyone wants an SUV.<P>A Fiero(or whatever Pontiac names it)like my previous GTR description in this post, would definitely be an image car. Price the line competitively with the competition and Pontiac would take a serious chunk out of that market share.<P>As the Vette pushes 50k and up, GM needs an affordable sports car. Hey, I already had  lots of people tell me they would buy a current version of the Fiero if it was availble. Some people on the Forum already want me to make up an order sheet for my GTR concept.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> How many more of you would buy a car like that if it were available? The line forms at the left!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Rick 88||||reg||4
Z||000037||BN Boomer||10-05-2001||09:05 PM||||What people say and what they do are often quite different. I'd bet that just a tiny fraction of the people who claim they would buy a new Fiero would actually do so. As soon as it was available, we would start hearing the inevitable excuse for backing away from their commitment. I can hear it now. "It's too expensive." "I don't like xxx about it." "They screwed xxx up." "I can't get it with xxx." It would be just like the time so many members here said they wanted to help the Fiero owner who was involved in an accident in Colorado but when it came time to step up to the plate, everyone was suddenly short of money, waiting for a paycheck, had bills, or some other sadsack excuse. <P>Here's an idea. Chevrolet rebadged the Toyota Camry as a Nova and nobody had any problems. They also simply renamed the Caprice Classic as a Impala SS and most people foolishly swallowed it as a muscle car. Pontiac could combine those two marketing tactics and simply rebadge the MR2 as a Fiero. I bet they'd sell a million of them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000038||GTFiero1||10-06-2001||11:55 AM||||The Impala SS isn't a re-badged caprice. The impala has more horsepower, better handling and braking and different interior and exterior traits. the impala is a caprice cop car for the public.<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000039||GTFiero1||10-06-2001||12:02 PM||||BN Boomer,<BR>  as of now my dad cant afford to by another impala yet. when he can he wants a 96 though. and the impalas are performance cars. of course not the 70's ones because performance cars were extinct due to the gas crisis. remember the 60's impala SS with the 427 big block, your so fine my 409. all impala. My dad had owned a 69 Chevelle SS with a 396 , a 70 chevelle SS with a 454, 77 monte Carlo, Pontiac Catalina, Caprice Classic, Cavalier Z24, Cadillac Coupe Deville, another Cadillac Coupe Deville and he had more....but he says the 94 Impala SS is the best car he had ever driven and owned. if he had the money he would have a 96<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
