Z||000041||Back On Holiday||10-06-2001||08:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTFiero1:<BR><B>As for the return of the Impala, I just bought one and beg to differ with those who ignorantly claim that it is the opposite of what it "originally stood for". Nah my dad had a 1994 Impala SS (only 6,303 made)everything thats on the caprice cop cars roaming around your town this car has. Plus edelbrock headers, intake, cat back exhaust, cold air intake, mass air-flow sensor and some more. This was a cool car<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Impala would be one of a few cars I would ever think of buying to replace the Family car.  <BR>on a side note, the state and local cops are starting to pick them up as unmarked cars.  Saw 3 of them along with 4 marked (other make)cars getting off the bypass friday and they were flying. Dont know where they were going.  They have 1 Red Mustang as a chase car...wonder whats under that hood?<BR><P>------------------<BR>84se Silver 2m4<BR>86se Silver 2m6||||reg||1
Z||000042||BN Boomer||10-08-2001||03:53 AM||||Yes, despite your objections, the 94-96 Impala SS <B>was</B> essentially just a rebadged Caprice Classic. There wasn't anything all that special about the car.  <P>I suppose you would also tell me that a Caddilac Escalade is not simply a gussied up Chevy Tahoe.<P>And good luck getting any sort of decent handling or braking performance out of an old Impala. They may have been good for lighting up the tires at the stoplight (been there, done that), but that's about it. <P>------------------<BR>Greg, aka Boomer<BR>Red 85 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.gmemd.com/locomotives/naf/images/sd70sm.gif"> ||||reg||1
